#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ad93e2e-20ab-5179-90b2-f96a357bd94c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Sums of Exponentials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57da5928-7d60-5fba-813c-dc9b71993f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2s}{(s-3)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338023} {:answer/id #uuid "a7168acc-33ea-5634-8b11-6ea013081509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{(s-1)(s+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338024} {:answer/id #uuid "2484cd56-f6c2-5b82-bc38-47b11f42636d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{(s-3)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338025} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b3d8cd-b6a5-5fdf-9e47-b6797cb172c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+1}{(s-3)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338026} {:answer/id #uuid "debb1db8-243d-5dd4-9609-bfbfb4ecdb72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{(s-3)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "debb1db8-243d-5dd4-9609-bfbfb4ecdb72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{(s-3)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338027} :answer-field/id #uuid "eada0007-4de9-5471-be45-58c5eb4925dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fde1c354-d593-5da3-9167-ee1bf6674333" :question/math-academy-id "q-331457" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{3t}-{e}^{-t}$ for $s>3.$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}-{e}^{-t}} & =L{{e}^{3t}}+L{-{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{3t}}-L{{e}^{-t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-3},\\,s>3 \\\\ L{{e}^{-t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+1},\\,s>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{3t}}-L{{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-3}-\\frac{1}{s+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s+1)-(s-3)}{(s-3)(s+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+1-s+3}{(s-3)(s+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(s-3)(s+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{3t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (3,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-1,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(3,\\infty )\\cap (-1,\\infty )=(3,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f97501c-2fa0-5b11-84b4-86faae530ce4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Combined Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e61cdf0a-5af5-5ec3-84ca-7f628dd0375b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{30-3s}{s^2+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e61cdf0a-5af5-5ec3-84ca-7f628dd0375b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{30-3s}{s^2+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338030} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fabe243-f221-5137-8d30-a1130f916221" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0bf4c6b3-b0c5-5df4-8aa1-f94e26ca379d" :question/math-academy-id "q-331505" :question/problem "Given that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t} & =L{-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t}+L{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t} \\\\  & =-3L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t}+6L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t} & =\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =-3L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t}+6L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25}+6\\cdot \\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3s}{{s}^{2}+25}+\\frac{30}{{s}^{2}+25} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3s+30}{{s}^{2}+25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c086bc97-415d-52c9-b26a-02dc8aebef33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Trigonometric and Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "968f1c52-6daa-5ebb-8da5-c398a5bb68f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338033} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a0b663-3082-539e-b4ea-e8ce7256f18e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338034} {:answer/id #uuid "18d4cdb5-1632-5d54-8694-6f3e6b506e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3s}{{s}^{2}-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338035} {:answer/id #uuid "c2384a2d-9f7d-5d13-b038-f10287be09ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338036} {:answer/id #uuid "86614287-4424-53a5-98e9-994b4cb9ef71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86614287-4424-53a5-98e9-994b4cb9ef71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338037} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f915ee1-3ad6-5e9b-8905-49ba2e7d2000" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338032} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e93b1b9-f87f-5b9a-a7a9-84b3da1d3e84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338039} {:answer/id #uuid "1ecc43ca-0d9a-5a06-83db-dceb642f5b46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338040} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca4af74-49b4-55cc-b4eb-83e9de43b712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338041} {:answer/id #uuid "f390ce37-8aea-55c8-a629-3c7775f96821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338042} {:answer/id #uuid "ea643c40-ecff-588f-a522-a8fae6a91314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338043} {:answer/id #uuid "93777995-9014-55b6-a4b3-5949842b771b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e93b1b9-f87f-5b9a-a7a9-84b3da1d3e84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338039} :answer-field/id #uuid "3af33b30-52da-5990-97de-e9b12f645781" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42bf59e6-fc11-5187-a3f2-889c117ed67e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340655" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)=\\frac{{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{3t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{3t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-3t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-3},\\,s>3 \\\\ L{{e}^{-3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3},\\,s>-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{3t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-3}+\\frac{1}{s+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+3)+(s-3)}{(s-3)(s+3)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{3t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (3,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-3t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-3,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(3,\\infty )\\cap (-3,\\infty )=(3,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c086bc97-415d-52c9-b26a-02dc8aebef33" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Trigonometric and Hyperbolic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5604f237-d4f6-556b-b420-7ced80e5606e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338047} {:answer/id #uuid "9f825f7b-e269-5476-b7b2-2576efc6012f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338048} {:answer/id #uuid "ad822efe-34a3-57a6-80e3-30081ce83138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}-25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338049} {:answer/id #uuid "1fc70b66-19eb-5d4e-819e-1948cc949e8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5s}{{s}^{2}+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338050} {:answer/id #uuid "31f55d77-623b-5b53-b922-3079b7ae3899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5604f237-d4f6-556b-b420-7ced80e5606e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338047} :answer-field/id #uuid "06b49f43-2c2c-5bf5-a295-ac9a81108243" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338046} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a96be325-2dc6-523b-a091-ba2451fc619c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge -5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338053} {:answer/id #uuid "47c1cfbf-4a79-54b4-9d75-7f6b76430b6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338054} {:answer/id #uuid "d5411ce2-2e3d-5588-b1da-ce6b3b74b5fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338055} {:answer/id #uuid "d73cf164-bb68-5ac6-8bd9-3b0ba81dbcd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338056} {:answer/id #uuid "26136647-8c33-5ec4-8250-91979aa131f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338057} {:answer/id #uuid "790b00f9-f81c-5bba-a1e7-e346ae1bf778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "790b00f9-f81c-5bba-a1e7-e346ae1bf778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338058} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccd04c2c-74c6-5b7b-9f18-88255c0e4f63" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81b1c410-ca7f-529a-81f8-133edb7ade0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-341137" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that the cosine function can be defined in terms of complex exponentials as follows: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)=\\frac{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}+{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}}{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}+{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}}=\\frac{1}{s-\\text{i}\\omega },\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-5\\text{i}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+5\\text{i}},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-5\\text{i}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-5\\text{i}}+\\frac{1}{s+5\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+5\\text{i})+(s-5\\text{i})}{(s-5\\text{i})(s+5\\text{i})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f97501c-2fa0-5b11-84b4-86faae530ce4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Combined Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e8ae64-d43b-5769-8c35-24cbd1416cc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{1}{2({s}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338061} {:answer/id #uuid "ffde2444-0148-5c4e-a083-f043358edc2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338062} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6265bd-b8cf-56e2-909e-d8f455050b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2s}{({s}^{2}+4)({s}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338063} {:answer/id #uuid "445fefe1-8dbe-5fc9-b8ee-0cbe79f0b64d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s+2}{({s}^{2}+4)({s}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338064} {:answer/id #uuid "77f12708-2721-5ec7-a25e-0fa1584c1fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+2}+\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffde2444-0148-5c4e-a083-f043358edc2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338062} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb64e9c6-7a35-54bd-a670-1c86460dd9fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12d33544-2264-5fb1-9577-bd37cfd5a454" :question/math-academy-id "q-89596" :question/problem "Given that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ for $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}+L{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}+2L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}+2L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
